% ============================================================================
% flame-colors — each metal (or metal ion) and the color it gives in a flame
% test, as the source states it (adj-facts-stdlib, CHEMISTRY).
% ============================================================================
% One of the very first "the world is made of elements, and each one has a
% fingerprint" ideas a chemistry student meets. Heat a salt in a hot flame and
% the metal in it glows a characteristic color: sodium glows orange, potassium
% violet, lithium red. The metal ions are lifted to an excited state by the heat
% of the flame and then fall back, emitting light of colors characteristic of
% that element — so the color you SEE names the metal that is present. A flame
% test is a recall: "what color does barium give?" is a binding query.
%
%     ? flame_color(sodium, $Color)      % orange
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `flame_color(metal, color)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the color
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a metal
% whose flame color this source does not state (it never invents a color).
%
% Each metal the source names and the flame color it states for it, as a little
% truth table:
%
%     metal       flame color (source's word)   a beginner's cue
%     ---------   ---------------------------   ------------------------------
%     barium      green                         "Barium gives a green flame"
%     strontium   red                           "Strontium gives a red flame"
%     lithium     red                           "Lithium gives a red flame"
%     calcium     red_orange                    "Calcium gives a red orange flame"
%     sodium      orange                        "Sodium gives an orange flame"
%     potassium   violet                        "Potassium give a violet flame"
%
% Notice two metals — strontium and lithium — both give `red`: a flame color is
% NOT unique to one element, so the relation is many-to-one. That makes the
% reverse recall genuinely interesting: binding a color runs the relation
% BACKWARD and can recall MORE than one metal.
%
%     ? flame_color($Metal, red)   % strontium ; lithium — one color, two metals
%
% A metal whose flame color is NOT stated on this source page — `copper`,
% `magnesium`, `iron` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a flame-color recall
% ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a color. That honest-abstention property
% is what makes the table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the
% metal→color pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `color` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, and it is the
% source's OWN color word: where the source says "red orange" (two words), the
% atom joins them into a single token `red_orange`, so every value stays
% literally checkable against the quoted span — never a color the source does
% not support.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every metal→color pair
% below was read out of a fetched University of Washington Department of
% Chemistry lecture-demonstrations page, and any pair whose color could not be
% verified there would have been dropped). Each pair, with the primary source it
% was copied from and the verbatim span that states it:
%
%   barium → green
%     https://chem.washington.edu/lecture-demos/flame-tests
%     (University of Washington — Department of Chemistry)
%     "Barium gives a green flame"
%
%   strontium → red
%     https://chem.washington.edu/lecture-demos/flame-tests
%     "Strontium gives a red flame"
%
%   lithium → red
%     https://chem.washington.edu/lecture-demos/flame-tests
%     "Lithium gives a red flame"
%
%   calcium → red_orange
%     https://chem.washington.edu/lecture-demos/flame-tests
%     "Calcium gives a red orange flame"
%
%   sodium → orange
%     https://chem.washington.edu/lecture-demos/flame-tests
%     "Sodium gives an orange flame"
%
%   potassium → violet
%     https://chem.washington.edu/lecture-demos/flame-tests
%     "Potassium give a violet flame"   (source's wording, verbatim)
%
% (Copper is a metal a student might expect here — it gives a green/blue-green
% flame — but THIS source page does not state a copper flame color, so copper is
% deliberately dropped rather than filled in from memory: a flame-color recall
% on copper abstains.)
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the statement that fixes the
% first row (barium → green). Every OTHER row's per-fact source and verbatim span
% is listed above, so each color remains independently checkable. The source is
% the University of Washington Department of Chemistry, a university chemistry
% department teaching resource, so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table flame_color {
    columns metal, color

    row (barium, green)
    row (strontium, red)
    row (lithium, red)
    row (calcium, red_orange)
    row (sodium, orange)
    row (potassium, violet)

    source "Barium gives a green flame"
    locator "https://chem.washington.edu/lecture-demos/flame-tests"
    trust authoritative
}
